{t:"read",part:"Part 7",passage:"INTERNAL MEMO\nTo: All Department Heads\nFrom: IT Services\nDate: July 9\nSubject: Printer Replacement on Floors 2 and 3\nThe old printers on floors 2 and 3 will be replaced with new network models during the weekend of July 18-19. From Monday, July 20, all print jobs must be sent to the new printers. Before then, staff must install a new printer driver on their computers; step-by-step instructions are attached to this memo. Old printer codes will stop working on July 17. If you experience any problems, please submit a ticket through the IT help desk.",q:"What must staff do before using the new printers?",o:["Install a new printer driver","Buy new paper","Move to a different floor","Submit a ticket to the IT help desk"],a:0,zh:"備忘錄要求員工先安裝新的印表機驅動程式（printer driver）。"},
{t:"read",part:"Part 7",passage:"INTERNAL MEMO\nTo: All Department Heads\nFrom: IT Services\nDate: July 9\nSubject: Printer Replacement on Floors 2 and 3\nThe old printers on floors 2 and 3 will be replaced with new network models during the weekend of July 18-19. From Monday, July 20, all print jobs must be sent to the new printers. Before then, staff must install a new printer driver on their computers; step-by-step instructions are attached to this memo. Old printer codes will stop working on July 17. If you experience any problems, please submit a ticket through the IT help desk.",q:"When will the old printer codes stop working?",o:["On July 9","On July 17","On July 18","On July 20"],a:1,zh:"舊的列印代碼將於7月17日失效。"},
{t:"read",part:"Part 7",passage:"INTERNAL MEMO\nTo: All Department Heads\nFrom: IT Services\nDate: July 9\nSubject: Printer Replacement on Floors 2 and 3\nThe old printers on floors 2 and 3 will be replaced with new network models during the weekend of July 18-19. From Monday, July 20, all print jobs must be sent to the new printers. Before then, staff must install a new printer driver on their computers; step-by-step instructions are attached to this memo. Old printer codes will stop working on July 17. If you experience any problems, please submit a ticket through the IT help desk.",q:"When will the new printers be installed?",o:["On July 9","On July 17","Over the weekend of July 18-19","On Monday, July 20"],a:2,zh:"新印表機將於7月18至19日的週末期間安裝。"},
{t:"read",part:"Part 7",passage:"Hi Robert,\nHere is the plan for the trade fair in Berlin. Our flight, LH204, leaves Heathrow at 7:15 a.m. on Monday, October 6, and arrives at 9:30 a.m. A company car will meet you at the airport. We are staying at the Hotel Mitte, where your reservation is under the name 'R. Thompson' for three nights. The fair opens at 10:00 a.m. on Tuesday, and our booth is in Hall C, stand number 42. Please bring 500 product brochures with you.\nSee you at the gate,\nAnna",q:"How will Robert get from the airport?",o:["By hotel shuttle","By company car","By train","By taxi at his own expense"],a:1,zh:"Anna 會派公司車到機場接 Robert。"},
{t:"read",part:"Part 7",passage:"Hi Robert,\nHere is the plan for the trade fair in Berlin. Our flight, LH204, leaves Heathrow at 7:15 a.m. on Monday, October 6, and arrives at 9:30 a.m. A company car will meet you at the airport. We are staying at the Hotel Mitte, where your reservation is under the name 'R. Thompson' for three nights. The fair opens at 10:00 a.m. on Tuesday, and our booth is in Hall C, stand number 42. Please bring 500 product brochures with you.\nSee you at the gate,\nAnna",q:"Under which name is the hotel room reserved?",o:["Anna Thompson","Hotel Mitte","Robert Reed","R. Thompson"],a:3,zh:"飯店訂房登記的名字是 R. Thompson。"},
{t:"read",part:"Part 7",passage:"Hi Robert,\nHere is the plan for the trade fair in Berlin. Our flight, LH204, leaves Heathrow at 7:15 a.m. on Monday, October 6, and arrives at 9:30 a.m. A company car will meet you at the airport. We are staying at the Hotel Mitte, where your reservation is under the name 'R. Thompson' for three nights. The fair opens at 10:00 a.m. on Tuesday, and our booth is in Hall C, stand number 42. Please bring 500 product brochures with you.\nSee you at the gate,\nAnna",q:"What must Robert bring with him?",o:["500 product brochures","A taxi receipt","The booth key","Three nights' payment"],a:0,zh:"Anna 要求 Robert 準備500份產品手冊。"},
{t:"read",part:"Part 7",passage:"Many office workers believe that a long lunch break makes them less productive, but research suggests the opposite. A study of 600 employees found that those who took a proper break away from their desks returned to work with more energy and made fewer errors. Interestingly, the length of the break mattered less than the activity: workers who walked outside or chatted with colleagues reported greater benefits than those who continued working while eating at their desks. The authors therefore recommend that companies actively encourage staff to leave their desks during the lunch hour.\nSource: Workplace Health Journal, May issue",q:"What did the study find about employees who took proper breaks?",o:["They made fewer mistakes","They worked longer hours","They ate at their desks","They received higher pay"],a:0,zh:"研究發現離開座位好好休息的員工回工作崗位後犯錯較少。"},
{t:"read",part:"Part 7",passage:"Many office workers believe that a long lunch break makes them less productive, but research suggests the opposite. A study of 600 employees found that those who took a proper break away from their desks returned to work with more energy and made fewer errors. Interestingly, the length of the break mattered less than the activity: workers who walked outside or chatted with colleagues reported greater benefits than those who continued working while eating at their desks. The authors therefore recommend that companies actively encourage staff to leave their desks during the lunch hour.\nSource: Workplace Health Journal, May issue",q:"According to the study, what mattered more than the length of the break?",o:["The time of day","The cost of the meal","What employees did during it","Where the company is located"],a:2,zh:"休息時間長短不如休息時從事的活動重要。"},
{t:"read",part:"Part 7",passage:"Many office workers believe that a long lunch break makes them less productive, but research suggests the opposite. A study of 600 employees found that those who took a proper break away from their desks returned to work with more energy and made fewer errors. Interestingly, the length of the break mattered less than the activity: workers who walked outside or chatted with colleagues reported greater benefits than those who continued working while eating at their desks. The authors therefore recommend that companies actively encourage staff to leave their desks during the lunch hour.\nSource: Workplace Health Journal, May issue",q:"What do the authors recommend?",o:["Shortening the lunch hour","Encouraging staff to leave their desks","Allowing employees to work while eating","Offering free meals in the office"],a:1,zh:"作者建議公司鼓勵員工午餐時間離開座位。"},
{t:"read",part:"Part 7",passage:"Dear Customer Services,\nI am writing to complain about order number 4482, which arrived on September 2. Three of the twelve chairs were damaged in transit, and one was missing completely. I telephoned your office on September 3 and was told that a replacement would be sent within five working days, but nothing has arrived. As we need the chairs for a conference on September 20, I would appreciate delivery by September 15 at the latest. Otherwise, I would like a full refund for the missing chair.\nYours faithfully,\nHelen Okafor, Events Manager, Brightline Ltd.",q:"What problem is the writer reporting?",o:["The wrong chairs were delivered","Some chairs were damaged and one is missing","The delivery arrived a month late","The invoice was sent to the wrong address"],a:1,zh:"12張椅子中3張在運送中損壞、1張完全遺失。"},
{t:"read",part:"Part 7",passage:"Dear Customer Services,\nI am writing to complain about order number 4482, which arrived on September 2. Three of the twelve chairs were damaged in transit, and one was missing completely. I telephoned your office on September 3 and was told that a replacement would be sent within five working days, but nothing has arrived. As we need the chairs for a conference on September 20, I would appreciate delivery by September 15 at the latest. Otherwise, I would like a full refund for the missing chair.\nYours faithfully,\nHelen Okafor, Events Manager, Brightline Ltd.",q:"What did the company promise on September 3?",o:["To send a replacement within five working days","To offer a discount on the next order","To collect the damaged chairs immediately","To confirm the conference date"],a:0,zh:"9月3日電話中公司承諾5個工作日內補寄，但至今未到。"},
{t:"read",part:"Part 7",passage:"Dear Customer Services,\nI am writing to complain about order number 4482, which arrived on September 2. Three of the twelve chairs were damaged in transit, and one was missing completely. I telephoned your office on September 3 and was told that a replacement would be sent within five working days, but nothing has arrived. As we need the chairs for a conference on September 20, I would appreciate delivery by September 15 at the latest. Otherwise, I would like a full refund for the missing chair.\nYours faithfully,\nHelen Okafor, Events Manager, Brightline Ltd.",q:"What does the writer want if the chairs do not arrive in time?",o:["A new order at half price","A full refund for the missing chair","An apology by email","A free conference ticket"],a:1,zh:"若無法準時送達，她要求對遺失的那張椅子全額退款。"},
{t:"read",part:"Part 7",passage:"Although passenger numbers at regional airports have grown steadily since 2021, many of the smaller airports continue to lose money. The main difficulty is that airlines prefer to concentrate their flights on a few large hubs, where they can fill bigger planes more easily. Regional authorities have responded by offering subsidies to carriers that operate routes to smaller towns. Early results are promising: one airport in the north reported a 27 percent rise in passengers after a subsidy was introduced. However, critics argue that public money would be better spent on improving rail links.\nFinancial Times, March 12",q:"Why do airlines prefer large hubs?",o:["They can fill larger planes more easily","They pay no landing fees","They receive government subsidies","They are closer to regional towns"],a:0,zh:"航空公司偏好大型樞紐，因為較容易讓大飛機坐滿。"},
{t:"read",part:"Part 7",passage:"Although passenger numbers at regional airports have grown steadily since 2021, many of the smaller airports continue to lose money. The main difficulty is that airlines prefer to concentrate their flights on a few large hubs, where they can fill bigger planes more easily. Regional authorities have responded by offering subsidies to carriers that operate routes to smaller towns. Early results are promising: one airport in the north reported a 27 percent rise in passengers after a subsidy was introduced. However, critics argue that public money would be better spent on improving rail links.\nFinancial Times, March 12",q:"How have regional authorities tried to attract flights?",o:["By raising ticket prices","By improving rail links","By offering subsidies","By building bigger hubs"],a:2,zh:"地方當局以補貼（subsidies）吸引航空公司開闢小鎮航線。"},
{t:"read",part:"Part 7",passage:"Although passenger numbers at regional airports have grown steadily since 2021, many of the smaller airports continue to lose money. The main difficulty is that airlines prefer to concentrate their flights on a few large hubs, where they can fill bigger planes more easily. Regional authorities have responded by offering subsidies to carriers that operate routes to smaller towns. Early results are promising: one airport in the north reported a 27 percent rise in passengers after a subsidy was introduced. However, critics argue that public money would be better spent on improving rail links.\nFinancial Times, March 12",q:"What do critics believe the public money should be spent on?",o:["Subsidizing more airlines","Improving rail links","Expanding regional airports","Promoting air travel"],a:1,zh:"批評者主張公共資金應用於改善鐵路連結（rail links）。"},
{t:"read",part:"Part 7",passage:"From: j.mori@globalfreight.co.jp\nTo: s.diaz@vantage-retail.com\nSubject: RE: Container Shipment VN-221\nHi Sofia,\nThe container is now on the vessel Pacific Star, which departed Yokohama on April 4 and is due to arrive in Long Beach on April 18. Customs clearance usually takes two working days, so the goods should be ready for collection on April 22. Please arrange the truck by then, because the terminal charges an extra fee for storage after five days. I have attached the bill of lading and the packing list for your records.\nBest regards,\nJun Mori",q:"When should the goods be ready for collection?",o:["On April 4","On April 18","On April 20","On April 22"],a:3,zh:"貨船4月18日抵港，報關約需兩個工作日，4月22日即可提貨。"},
{t:"read",part:"Part 7",passage:"From: j.mori@globalfreight.co.jp\nTo: s.diaz@vantage-retail.com\nSubject: RE: Container Shipment VN-221\nHi Sofia,\nThe container is now on the vessel Pacific Star, which departed Yokohama on April 4 and is due to arrive in Long Beach on April 18. Customs clearance usually takes two working days, so the goods should be ready for collection on April 22. Please arrange the truck by then, because the terminal charges an extra fee for storage after five days. I have attached the bill of lading and the packing list for your records.\nBest regards,\nJun Mori",q:"Why should the truck be arranged by April 22?",o:["The terminal adds storage fees after five days","The vessel leaves on that day","Customs officers work only on Mondays","The bill of lading expires"],a:0,zh:"貨櫃碼頭超過五天會加收倉儲費，因此須準時安排卡車。"},
{t:"read",part:"Part 7",passage:"When the city council announced plans to close the Riverside swimming pool last year, residents reacted with surprise and anger. Built in 1974, the pool had been used by schools and sports clubs for decades, and many families depended on it for affordable exercise. The council argued that repairs to the leaking roof would cost more than the pool could earn in entrance fees. In response, a local campaign group collected 4,200 signatures and organized a swim-in on a cold Sunday morning. Since then, the council has agreed to keep the pool open until at least 2028 while it looks for a private operator to take over its management.",q:"Why did the council want to close the pool?",o:["It was too old to be used","Repairs would cost more than the income it produced","Local families opposed it","Schools refused to pay fees"],a:1,zh:"市議會認為屋頂維修費會高於門票收入，因此想關閉泳池。"},
{t:"read",part:"Part 7",passage:"When the city council announced plans to close the Riverside swimming pool last year, residents reacted with surprise and anger. Built in 1974, the pool had been used by schools and sports clubs for decades, and many families depended on it for affordable exercise. The council argued that repairs to the leaking roof would cost more than the pool could earn in entrance fees. In response, a local campaign group collected 4,200 signatures and organized a swim-in on a cold Sunday morning. Since then, the council has agreed to keep the pool open until at least 2028 while it looks for a private operator to take over its management.",q:"How did residents respond to the announcement?",o:["They signed a petition and held a swim-in","They voted out the council members","They stopped using the pool","They asked schools to take over"],a:0,zh:"居民發起連署（4,200個簽名）並舉辦泳池快閃活動以示抗議。"},
{t:"read",part:"Part 7",passage:"When the city council announced plans to close the Riverside swimming pool last year, residents reacted with surprise and anger. Built in 1974, the pool had been used by schools and sports clubs for decades, and many families depended on it for affordable exercise. The council argued that repairs to the leaking roof would cost more than the pool could earn in entrance fees. In response, a local campaign group collected 4,200 signatures and organized a swim-in on a cold Sunday morning. Since then, the council has agreed to keep the pool open until at least 2028 while it looks for a private operator to take over its management.",q:"What has the council decided since then?",o:["To close the pool in 2028","To hand the pool to schools immediately","To keep it open until at least 2028","To build a new pool in the north"],a:2,zh:"市議會已同意泳池至少營運到2028年，同時尋找民間營運者。"}
